# Retry Function (Synchronous)

Here's a function that retries a callback up to N times if it throws:

```javascript
/**
 * Retries a callback up to `retries` times if it throws.
 *
 * @param {Function} callback - The function to execute.
 * @param {number} retries - Maximum number of retry attempts.
 * @param {Object} [options]
 * @param {number} [options.delay=0] - Delay in ms between retries.
 * @param {Function} [options.onRetry] - Called on each failure with (error, attempt).
 * @param {Function} [options.shouldRetry] - Predicate to decide if a retry is allowed.
 * @returns {*} The callback's return value on success.
 * @throws The last error if all attempts fail.
 */
function retry(callback, retries, options = {}) {
  const { delay = 0, onRetry, shouldRetry = () => true } = options;
  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= retries + 1; attempt++) {
    try {
      return callback();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;

      const isLastAttempt = attempt === retries + 1;
      if (isLastAttempt || !shouldRetry(err, attempt)) {
        break;
      }

      if (onRetry) onRetry(err, attempt);

      if (delay > 0) {
        // Busy-wait is bad; better to make this async (see below).
        const start = Date.now();
        while (Date.now() - start < delay) {}
      }
    }
  }

  throw lastError;
}
```

## Usage

```javascript
let calls = 0;
const flaky = () => {
  calls++;
  if (calls < 3) throw new Error(`Attempt ${calls} failed`);
  return 'success!';
};

console.log(retry(flaky, 5)); // "success!"
```

## Async Version (Recommended)

Synchronous busy-waiting for delays is bad practice. If you need delays, use the async version:

```javascript
